findings.detail:
D:\FYP\frontend\src\components\ui\tabs.tsx reads:

  function Tabs({ className, orientation = "horizontal", ...props }: TabsPrimitive.Root.Props) {
    return <TabsPrimitive.Root data-slot="tabs" data-orientation={orientation} className={…} {...props} />
  }

`orientation` is destructured out and used ONLY for the styling hook `data-orientation`; it is never forwarded to `TabsPrimitive.Root`. Base UI derives arrow-key navigation (Left/Right vs Up/Down) and its own `data-orientation` from the real `orientation` prop, so `<Tabs orientation="vertical">` renders the vertical layout (the `group-data-vertical/tabs:*` utilities fire) while keyboard navigation stays horizontal. This is a keyboard-accessibility regression, not just cosmetic.

D:\FYP\frontend\src\lib\api.ts creates the instance with `headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" }`. Confirmed in the installed axios 1.20.0 source, D:\FYP\frontend\node_modules\axios\dist\node\axios.cjs, `transformRequest` (lines ~2385-2392):

    const contentType = headers.getContentType() || '';
    const hasJSONContentType = contentType.indexOf('application/json') > -1;
    const isFormData = utils$1.isFormData(data);
    if (isFormData) {
      return hasJSONContentType ? JSON.stringify(formDataToJSON(data)) : data;
    }

So `api.post('/wardrobe/items/', formData)` does NOT upload a file — axios runs the FormData through `formDataToJSON` and posts a JSON body in which the `File` serialises to `{}`. There is no error, no warning: Django just receives a request with no file. This will look like a backend bug and cost hours. Two supporting facts: axios sets `application/json` automatically for plain-object payloads anyway (`if (isObjectPayload || hasJSONContentType) { headers.setContentType('application/json', false); return stringifySafely(data) }`), so the global header buys nothing; and per-request `{ 'Content-Type': undefined }` genuinely removes it, because `normalizeValue` passes `undefined` through and `AxiosHeaders.toJSON` skips it (`value != null && value !== false && …`).

findings.actionable:
Write one upload helper in src\lib\api.ts and use it everywhere:

  export function uploadForm<T>(url: string, form: FormData, opts: { onProgress?: (pct: number) => void; signal?: AbortSignal; timeout?: number } = {}) {
    return api.post<T>(url, form, {
      signal: opts.signal,
      timeout: opts.timeout ?? 120_000,
      onUploadProgress: (e) => { if (e.total) opts.onProgress?.(Math.round((e.loaded * 100) / e.total)) },
    })
  }

Never pass a Content-Type. Drive a `<Progress>` from the percentage, and wire the dialog's cancel button to an `AbortController` so a half-finished photo upload can be abandoned. Don't override the adapter.
